.TH ilper 1 "May 2026" "ilper 2.5" "HP-IL Peripherals Emulator"
.SH NAME
ilper \- HP-IL peripherals emulator for the PILBox
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ilper
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ilper
emulates a set of HP-IL peripherals for use with the PILBox, a USB/HP-IL
converter designed by Jean-François Garnier.
It is intended for use with HP-IL controllers such as the HP-41C/CV/CX,
HP-75C and HP-71B.
.PP
On startup,
.B ilper
reads its configuration file, opens the terminal in full-screen mode and
automatically starts the HP-IL emulation.
The top line of the screen is a status bar showing the HP-IL address of each
active device, and the indicator
.B [SCOPE]
when the scope log file is open.
A separator line is drawn below the status bar.
The remaining lines form the display terminal window, sized to the terminal.
.PP
To exit, send SIGINT (Ctrl-C) or SIGTERM.
.SH EMULATED DEVICES
Between two and four HP-IL peripherals are emulated, depending on the configuration:
.TP
.B Display terminal (address 1, AID 0x32)
A terminal whose size adapts to the terminal window (minimum 80 columns).
The scroll buffer holds up to 100 lines.
Addresses are assigned by AAU (fixed) or AAD (sequential auto-address).
.br
Accepts the following ESC sequences from the HP-IL controller:
.nf

  ESC A   Cursor up
  ESC B   Cursor down
  ESC C   Cursor right
  ESC D   Cursor left
  ESC E   Clear device (clear buffer, cursor home)
  ESC H   Cursor home
  ESC J   Clear from cursor to end of buffer
  ESC Q   Insert cursor (bar cursor shape)
  ESC R   Replace cursor (block cursor shape)
  ESC S   Roll up (scroll window down)
  ESC T   Roll down (scroll window up)
  ESC <   Cursor off
  ESC >   Cursor on
  ESC %   Cursor to address (two bytes follow: column, row)
.fi
.IP
The SST (send status) response consists of two bytes.
The first byte is always 0x80.
The second byte has the following bit layout (bit 7 is always 0):
.nf

  bit 6,5   RAM test result (always 0 = good)
  bit 4     cursor mode: 0 = replace, 1 = insert
  bit 3     cursor: 0 = displayed, 1 = not displayed
  bit 2     video output: 0 = NTSC, 1 = PAM (always 0)
  bit 1     character set: 0 = inverse, 1 = roman8
  bit 0     screen width: 0 = 80 columns, 1 = 40 columns (always 0)
.fi
.IP
When keyboard forwarding is enabled (see
.BR keyboard ),
keystrokes are translated as follows before being sent to the controller:
.nf

  Arrow keys      ESC A / ESC B / ESC C / ESC D
  Return          CR (0x0D)
  Escape          ESC ESC
  Backspace       ESC i
  Other keys      sent as-is
.fi
.IP
The rendering of characters with codes 128\(en255 is controlled by the
.B display_encoding
configuration key.
With
.B roman8
(the default), characters are interpreted as HP Roman-8 and converted
to Unicode for display.
With
.BR inverse ,
the low 7 bits of each byte are displayed in reverse video.
.TP
.B Mass storage 1 (address 2)
A LIF disk image emulation.
The path to the LIF image file is set by the
.B LIF_file1
key in the configuration file.
If absent, no drive is present on the HP-IL loop.
.TP
.B Mass storage 2 (address 3)
A second LIF disk image emulation.
The path to the LIF image file is set by the
.B LIF_file2
key in the configuration file.
If absent, the second drive is not present on the HP-IL loop.
.B LIF_file2
requires
.B LIF_file1
to be set.
.TP
.B Printer (address 4, AID 0x22 \(em 80 columns, HP Escape sequences, no graphics)
A dumb printer that logs its output to the file specified by the
.B printer
key in the configuration file.
If absent or empty, printer output is discarded.
HP Roman-8 characters above 127 are converted to UTF-8 in the log.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B ilper
reads its configuration from
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ilper/ilper.conf
(defaulting to
.IR ~/.config/ilper/ilper.conf ).
The configuration file is mandatory: the program exits if it cannot be opened.
Lines beginning with
.B #
are comments. Each setting has the form
.IR key = value "  # optional comment"
Unknown keys are treated as errors.
.PP
Paths in the configuration file may be absolute, relative to
.IR $HOME ,
or use a leading
.B ~
as an alias for
.IR $HOME .
.PP
Recognised keys:
.TP
.B device
Serial port to which the PILBox is connected.
This key is mandatory.
.TP
.B LIF_file1
Path to the first LIF disk image file.
The file must exist and be readable and writable.
If absent, no mass-storage drive is present on the HP-IL loop.
.TP
.B LIF_file2
Path to the second LIF disk image file.
The file must exist and be readable and writable.
Requires
.B LIF_file1
to be set.
If absent, only one drive is emulated.
.TP
.B baudrate
Serial baud rate:
.B 115200
or
.BR 230400 .
This key is mandatory.
.TP
.B keyboard
Forward Mac keyboard input to the HP calculator.
Accepted values:
.B on
(enables COFI PILBox mode, IDY frames forwarded to the host) or
.BR off .
This key is mandatory.
.TP
.B display_encoding
Character encoding for bytes 128\(en255 on the display terminal.
.B roman8
(default): HP Roman-8 converted to Unicode.
.BR inverse :
the low 7 bits of each byte are displayed in reverse video.
This key is optional; the default is
.BR roman8 .
.TP
.B scope
Path to the HP-IL frame log file.
If absent or empty, frame logging is disabled.
.TP
.B printer
Path to the printer output log file.
If absent or empty, printer output is discarded.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/ilper/ilper.conf
Configuration file (mandatory).
.TP
.I <LIF_file1 path>
First LIF disk image, if configured.
.TP
.I <LIF_file2 path>
Second LIF disk image, if configured.
.TP
.I <printer path>
Printer output log, if configured.
.TP
.I <scope path>
HP-IL frame log, if configured.
.SH NOTES
The PILBox requires an FTDI USB serial driver.
Recent Linux distributions load it automatically when the device is plugged in.
On macOS, the built-in
.I AppleUSBFTDI
driver (macOS 10.14 and later) may be used.
.PP
The HP82160A HP-IL module is required to use the PILBox with the HP-41C/CV/CX.
.PP
The cursor shape sequences (ESC Q, ESC R) require a terminal emulator that
supports DECSCUSR (e.g. Ghostty, iTerm2, most xterm-compatible terminals).
.SH BUGS
The HP-IL addresses of the emulated peripherals are fixed by default
(display at 1, mass storage 1 at 2, mass storage 2 at 3, printer at 4)
when assigned by AAU.
When assigned by AAD (sequential auto-address), addresses depend on the
physical order of devices in the HP-IL loop.
